        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best walking pad on Amazon?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people, the WalkingPad Z1: a 47.6-inch walking area and a frame that folds in half, at $299 on Amazon when we checked, sold by its maker. Its 242 lb limit is the catch. Heavier walkers should look at the 400 lb MERACH W50 TrekPad, and for incline, UREVO’s CyberPad tilts to 14%.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best cheap walking pad on Amazon?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              UREVO’s flat Smart Walking Pad, which Amazon showed at $134.99 on 6 November 2026. Its listing gives a 35.43 by 15-inch belt, 0.6 to 4 mph and a 265 lb limit. The short belt suits slow desk walking rather than a brisk pace; if you want a handle for a little more, UREVO’s Strol 2E was about $170.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it safe to buy a walking pad on Amazon?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, if you check the seller and the model. A pad sold by Amazon or the brand’s own storefront carries the maker’s warranty and Amazon’s return process. Check the model number against the maker’s page, search the CPSC database for the brand, and avoid Sperax pads, which the CPSC told owners to stop using in April 2026.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you return a walking pad to Amazon?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually, within the window on the listing; Amazon says most items it sells can be returned within 30 days of delivery, with exceptions for some oversized items. The Z1 listing we checked gave 30 days but warned a return shipping fee may apply. Keep the packaging and test the pad properly in the first week.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which walking pad on Amazon has incline and a handle?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sunny’s Treadpad with 7% auto incline combines a powered 7% incline with a fold-up handlebar that unlocks 6 mph, for about $487 on Amazon on 6 November 2026. For less, the UREVO Strol 2E Pro pairs a fold-up rail with 12% incline set by hand, at $198 to $220 on Amazon the same day.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it better to buy a walking pad on Amazon or from the brand?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on the brand. UREVO’s own store gives only 14 days for unused returns and Sunny charges a 15% restocking fee on direct returns, so Amazon’s window can be the safer way to try one. WalkingPad’s store adds 15-day price protection that Amazon does not, and MERACH’s page gave a longer warranty than its Amazon listing.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How many stars should a walking pad have on Amazon?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              There is no safe threshold, which is why we do not print star ratings. Listings pool reviews across versions and models, and old versions keep their reviews. Read recent three-star reviews first, look for the same fault reported independently several times, and check the specification against the maker’s own page.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
